    "question": {
      ":question/id": "fb66f925-c5bd-5086-957f-95f341c63183",
      ":question/problem": "Express\n\n$$\n6\\cos^{2}\\left(5^\\circ\\right)-6\\sin^{2}\\left(5^\\circ\\right)\n$$\n\nas a single trigonometric ratio.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Factor out the common coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&6\\cos^{2}\\left(5^\\circ\\right)-6\\sin^{2}\\left(5^\\circ\\right) \\\\\n&=6\\left(\\cos^{2}\\left(5^\\circ\\right)-\\sin^{2}\\left(5^\\circ\\right)\\right).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall the **double-angle formula for cosine**:\n\n$$\n\\cos^2 x-\\sin^2 x=\\cos(2x).\n$$\n\nSubstitute $x=5^\\circ$ and double the angle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&6\\left(\\cos^{2}\\left(5^\\circ\\right)-\\sin^{2}\\left(5^\\circ\\right)\\right) \\\\\n&=6\\cos\\left(2\\cdot 5^\\circ\\right) \\\\\n&=6\\cos\\left(10^\\circ\\right).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOnly the angle doubles; the common coefficient stays outside the cosine.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
